{
  "schema_version": 1,
  "number": 42,
  "title": "Prop 42 \u2014 Taxes on assets: what future options would be restricted?",
  "fps": 30,
  "frames": 5921,
  "checked": "2026-10-19",
  "narration": "asset://0c703a32abafb9355297fe578e96d03ce3b1b4c53a92fcd975ffd82801746bd1.wav",
  "prompter_words": "words-global.json",
  "script": [
    {
      "name": "The big picture",
      "narration": "Proposition forty-two restricts certain future taxes. It focuses on owning personal property, including financial assets, and on taxes applied retroactively. Personal property here generally means assets other than real estate. California already taxes income from assets, such as investment gains, and some forms of personal property already have taxes. It generally does not tax simply owning stocks. The proposal is primarily about restricting new taxes, not issuing a refund."
    },
    {
      "name": "An illustrative example",
      "narration": "Imagine a business owner reviewing the value of company assets. Owning equipment or shares is different from earning a profit. A rule against a new ownership tax does not, by itself, eliminate income taxes on profits or every tax the owner already pays. A budget analyst considers future options for raising revenue."
    },
    {
      "name": "How it would work",
      "narration": "The measure would prohibit new state taxes on ownership of financial assets and other personal property. It also limits certain retroactive taxes: taxes applied to activities or periods before the tax was adopted. For illustration, a tax enacted later but applied to income earned in an earlier period is retroactive. The measure\u2019s detailed rules determine what is prohibited; this is not a claim that every tax adjustment becomes impossible. The analyst does not attach a specific dollar saving or loss. Future revenue might rise less because some tax options would be unavailable. The effect depends on what lawmakers or voters would otherwise propose. Prop forty-two could also prevent Prop forty\u2019s wealth tax from taking effect if both pass and forty-two gets more yes votes. The conflict and its legal interpretation matter alongside the broader restrictions."
    },
    {
      "name": "Why someone might vote yes",
      "narration": "A voter might favor predictable rules for savings, investments, and business assets, or object to taxes that reach backward. They may also want to stop the proposed wealth tax. This is a choice about limiting government\u2019s future taxing authority."
    },
    {
      "name": "Why someone might vote no",
      "narration": "You might want future voters to retain more tax choices. Another voter could prefer deciding future tax proposals individually instead of restricting them in the constitution. They may want wealth-based revenue options during a funding shortfall, or support Prop forty. Keeping an option available does not mean every future tax must be adopted."
    },
    {
      "name": "What polling indicates",
      "narration": "In the September PPIC survey of likely voters, 54 percent said yes, 43 percent no, and 4 percent were unsure. These are dated survey results, not votes already cast. A later September Berkeley survey found 43 percent yes, 34 percent no, and 23 percent undecided. Question wording and methods differ, so comparing polls does not by itself establish a trend."
    },
    {
      "name": "Make your own decision",
      "narration": "Yes imposes the new limits; no retains the state\u2019s current options without itself creating a new tax. Visit our website for the fuller explanation, source links, and official voter guide. Compare the tradeoffs, then make your own informed choice."
    }
  ],
